Programming in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>2  Values and variables</H1>
<HR>
Programs work with <I>values</I>: numbers, pieces of text and so on. In this chapter you will learn how to
calculate with numbers, work with text, and keep values in <I>variables</I> so you can use them again. By
the end you will have written a program that adds up a shopping bill.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Trying things out</H2>
The best way to learn a programming language is to try things. Keep a scratch program - call it
<CODE>Try</CODE> - in your MyProgs directory, and whenever this book shows you something new, type it into
Try, save it and run it. Change it, and see what happens. You can't break anything.<P>
For example, put this in Try and run it:<P>
<PRE>
print(2 + 3);
print('2 + 3');
</PRE>
The first line prints <CODE>5</CODE>: JavaScript works out <CODE>2 + 3</CODE> and prints the answer. The
second prints <CODE>2 + 3</CODE>, because it is inside quotes: it is a string, a piece of text, and
JavaScript prints text exactly as it is.<P>
<CODE>print</CODE> can print several values at once. Separate them with commas and it puts a space between
them:<P>
<PRE>
print('The answer is', 6 * 7);
</PRE>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>Numbers and arithmetic</H2>
JavaScript can do all the arithmetic you would expect. These are the <I>operators</I> it uses:<P>
<PRE>
Operator  Meaning               Example    Result
   +      add                   7 + 2      9
   -      subtract              7 - 2      5
   *      multiply              7 * 2      14
   /      divide                7 / 2      3.5
   %      remainder             7 % 2      1
   **     to the power of       7 ** 2     49
</PRE>
Multiplication uses <CODE>*</CODE> rather than a cross, and division uses <CODE>/</CODE>. Dividing gives an
exact answer, with a decimal point if necessary: <CODE>7 / 2</CODE> is <CODE>3.5</CODE>, not 3.
<CODE>%</CODE> gives the remainder left over after dividing: <CODE>7 % 2</CODE> is 1, because 2 goes into 7
three times with 1 left over. That turns out to be surprisingly useful, as you'll see in the next
chapter.<P>
When a calculation has several operators, JavaScript follows the rules you learnt at school:
<CODE>**</CODE> first, then <CODE>*</CODE>, <CODE>/</CODE> and <CODE>%</CODE>, then <CODE>+</CODE> and
<CODE>-</CODE>. Brackets change the order:<P>
<PRE>
print(2 + 3 * 4);      // 14: the multiplication is done first
print((2 + 3) * 4);    // 20: the brackets are worked out first
</PRE>
If you aren't sure which way round JavaScript will do something, put brackets in. They do no harm, and
make the program easier to read.<P>
Numbers can have decimal points (<CODE>2.5</CODE>) and minus signs (<CODE>-40</CODE>). Don't use commas
in large numbers: write a million as <CODE>1000000</CODE>.<P>
<B>Note</B>: computers keep decimals in binary, so a few simple-looking sums come out very slightly wrong.
Try <CODE>print(0.1 + 0.2);</CODE> - it prints <CODE>0.30000000000000004</CODE>. This happens in almost
every programming language. When you show amounts of money you round them anyway, as you'll see later in this
chapter.<P>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Variables</H2>
A <I>variable</I> is a name for a value. You met one in chapter 1: <CODE>name</CODE> held your answer to a
question. You make a variable with <CODE>const</CODE>, give it a name, and use <CODE>=</CODE> to give it a
value:<P>
<PRE>
const price = 4;
const quantity = 3;
print(price * quantity);
</PRE>
Wherever the program uses the name, JavaScript uses the value, so this prints 12. Variables let you work
something out once and use the answer many times, and they make programs easier to read:
<CODE>price * quantity</CODE> says much more than <CODE>4 * 3</CODE>.<P>
<H3>Names</H3>
A variable's name can contain letters, digits, <CODE>_</CODE> and <CODE>$</CODE>, but can't start with a
digit or contain spaces. Upper and lower case are different, so <CODE>price</CODE> and <CODE>Price</CODE>
are two different variables. A few words, such as <CODE>const</CODE>, <CODE>if</CODE> and
<CODE>for</CODE>, belong to JavaScript itself and can't be used as names.<P>
Choose names that say what the value is. When a name is made of several words, the usual JavaScript style is
to run them together with a capital letter at the start of each word after the first:
<CODE>unitPrice</CODE>, <CODE>numberOfItems</CODE>. This is called <I>camel case</I>, because of the
humps.<P>
<H3>Changing a variable: let</H3>
A variable made with <CODE>const</CODE> (short for <I>constant</I>) keeps the same value for ever. If you
try to give it another value, you get an error:<P>
<PRE>
const score = 10;
score = 20;            // error: Assignment to constant variable.
</PRE>
When a value needs to change, make the variable with <CODE>let</CODE> instead:<P>
<PRE>
let score = 10;
print(score);          // 10
score = 20;
print(score);          // 20
score = score + 5;
print(score);          // 25
</PRE>
The last change is worth reading carefully. <CODE>=</CODE> doesn't mean "is equal to" as it does in
mathematics: it means "becomes". JavaScript works out the right-hand side first, using the score as it is
now (20), adds 5, and then makes that (25) the new value of <CODE>score</CODE>.<P>
You only write <CODE>let</CODE> or <CODE>const</CODE> the first time, when you make the variable. Writing
<CODE>let score</CODE> a second time gives the error <I>Identifier 'score' has already been declared</I>.<P>
Which should you use? Use <CODE>const</CODE> unless you know the value has to change; then use
<CODE>let</CODE>. That way JavaScript warns you if you change something by mistake. (Older JavaScript
programs use a third word, <CODE>var</CODE>. It still works, but it has some odd habits, and you don't need
it.)<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Strings</H2>
A string is a piece of text, written between quotes. You can use single quotes or double quotes, as long as
both ends match. That lets you put one kind of quote inside the other:<P>
<PRE>
print("It's raining.");
print('She said "hello".');
</PRE>
<CODE>+</CODE> joins strings together, as you saw in chapter 1. A string also knows how long it is, and can
make changed copies of itself:<P>
<PRE>
const town = 'Cambridge';
print(town.length);           // 9
print(town.toUpperCase());    // CAMBRIDGE
print('Ho! '.repeat(3));      // Ho! Ho! Ho!
</PRE>
The dot means "belonging to": <CODE>town.length</CODE> is the length of <CODE>town</CODE>, and
<CODE>town.toUpperCase()</CODE> asks <CODE>town</CODE> for an upper-case copy of itself. Things with brackets
after them, like <CODE>toUpperCase()</CODE>, are functions; things without, like <CODE>length</CODE>, are
values. The original string doesn't change: <CODE>town</CODE> is still <CODE>Cambridge</CODE>.<P>
<H3>Template strings</H3>
Joining strings and numbers with <CODE>+</CODE> quickly gets awkward:<P>
<PRE>
print('The ' + quantity + ' items cost ' + price * quantity + '.');
</PRE>
A <I>template string</I> is easier. It is written between back-quotes (<CODE>`</CODE>, the key at the top
left of most keyboards), and anything between <CODE>${</CODE> and <CODE>}</CODE> inside it is worked out and
put into the string:<P>
<PRE>
print(`The ${quantity} items cost ${price * quantity}.`);
</PRE>
Both print <CODE>The 3 items cost 12.</CODE>, but the second is much easier to write and to read. This book
uses template strings from now on whenever it puts values into text.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>Numbers from the keyboard</H2>
<CODE>input</CODE> always gives you a string, even when you type a number. That matters, because
<CODE>+</CODE> means two different things: with numbers it adds, but if either side is a string it joins.
So:<P>
<PRE>
const text = await input('Type a number: ');   // you type 5
print(text + 3);                                // 53
</PRE>
<CODE>'5' + 3</CODE> is <CODE>53</CODE>: the 3 is joined on to the end of the text. To turn a string into a
number, use <CODE>Number</CODE>:<P>
<PRE>
const n = Number(await input('Type a number: '));   // you type 5
print(n + 3);                                        // 8
</PRE>
If the string isn't a number at all - you typed <CODE>five</CODE>, say - <CODE>Number</CODE> gives a special
value, <CODE>NaN</CODE>, which stands for "not a number". Any sum involving <CODE>NaN</CODE> gives
<CODE>NaN</CODE> too, so if a program prints <CODE>NaN</CODE>, a number has come from somewhere it
shouldn't. The next chapter shows how a program can check for it.<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>A shopping bill</H2>
Here is a program that puts all of this together. It asks for two items, their prices and how many of each
you want, then prints a bill with VAT added. VAT is shown at 17.5%, the rate in 1997, when this computer
was new.<P>
<PRE>
// Bill - a shopping bill with VAT added
const VAT_RATE = 17.5;   // per cent: the rate in 1997

print('Shopping bill');
const item1 = await input('First item: ');
const price1 = Number(await input('Price of one, in pounds: '));
const quantity1 = Number(await input('How many? '));
const item2 = await input('Second item: ');
const price2 = Number(await input('Price of one, in pounds: '));
const quantity2 = Number(await input('How many? '));

const cost1 = price1 * quantity1;
const cost2 = price2 * quantity2;
const subtotal = cost1 + cost2;
const vat = subtotal * VAT_RATE / 100;
const total = subtotal + vat;

print('');
print(`${quantity1} x ${item1} at £${price1.toFixed(2)}`);
print(`  cost £${cost1.toFixed(2)}`);
print(`${quantity2} x ${item2} at £${price2.toFixed(2)}`);
print(`  cost £${cost2.toFixed(2)}`);
print(`Subtotal: £${subtotal.toFixed(2)}`);
print(`VAT at ${VAT_RATE}%: £${vat.toFixed(2)}`);
print(`Total: £${total.toFixed(2)}`);
</PRE>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/Bill" WIDTH=586 HEIGHT=349></CENTER>
Run it and answer the questions. Some points to notice:<P>
<UL>
<LI><CODE>VAT_RATE</CODE> is written in capitals. That is a common way of marking a value that is fixed
for the whole program and chosen by the programmer. If the rate changes, you only have to change one line.<P>
<LI><CODE>Number(await input(...))</CODE> asks the question and turns the answer into a number in one go.
JavaScript works from the inside out: first <CODE>input</CODE> asks, then <CODE>Number</CODE> converts the
answer.<P>
<LI>The calculations are done one step at a time, each kept in a variable with a sensible name. The whole
bill could be worked out in one enormous line, but nobody would be able to read it.<P>
<LI><CODE>toFixed(2)</CODE> turns a number into a string with exactly two digits after the decimal point,
rounding if it has to: <CODE>(0.45325).toFixed(2)</CODE> is <CODE>'0.45'</CODE>, and
<CODE>(1.1).toFixed(2)</CODE> is <CODE>'1.10'</CODE>. That is just what you want for money.<P>
<LI><CODE>print('')</CODE> prints an empty line, to space things out.<P>
</UL>
The pound sign is part of the RISC OS character set, so you can type it into JsEdit (Shift-3 on a UK keyboard) and it
prints normally.<P>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>Using the Math functions</H2>
JavaScript has a collection of mathematical functions and values called <CODE>Math</CODE>. You use them
with a dot, like the string functions:<P>
<PRE>
Math.PI          the number pi, 3.141592653589793
Math.sqrt(x)     the square root of x
Math.round(x)    x rounded to the nearest whole number
Math.floor(x)    x rounded down to a whole number
Math.abs(x)      x without its minus sign
Math.max(a, b)   the larger of a and b (Math.min: the smaller)
</PRE>
This program works out the area and circumference of a circle:<P>
<PRE>
// Circle - the area and circumference of a circle
const radius = Number(await input('Radius of the circle: '));

const area = Math.PI * radius ** 2;
const circumference = 2 * Math.PI * radius;
const side = Math.sqrt(area);

print(`Area: ${area.toFixed(2)}`);
print(`Circumference: ${circumference.toFixed(2)}`);
print(`Rounded: ${Math.round(area)}, ${Math.round(circumference)}`);
print(`A square of the same area has sides of ${side.toFixed(2)}.`);
</PRE>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/Circle" WIDTH=586 HEIGHT=349></CENTER>
<CODE>radius ** 2</CODE> is the radius squared, so the area is pi times the radius squared, as the
formula says. Because <CODE>**</CODE> is done before <CODE>*</CODE>, no brackets are needed.
<CODE>Math.round</CODE> gives whole numbers, and <CODE>Math.sqrt</CODE> finds how long the sides of a square
with the same area would be.<P>

<A NAME=S8></A>
<H2>Summary</H2>
<UL>
<LI>Numbers can be added, subtracted, multiplied (<CODE>*</CODE>), divided (<CODE>/</CODE>), raised to a
power (<CODE>**</CODE>), and <CODE>%</CODE> gives a remainder. Multiplication and division are done before
addition and subtraction; brackets change the order.<P>
<LI>A variable is a name for a value. Make it with <CODE>const</CODE> if it never changes, with
<CODE>let</CODE> if it does. <CODE>=</CODE> means "becomes".<P>
<LI>Strings can be joined with <CODE>+</CODE>, have a <CODE>length</CODE>, and have functions such as
<CODE>toUpperCase()</CODE> and <CODE>repeat(n)</CODE>.<P>
<LI>A template string, between back-quotes, puts values into text with <CODE>${...}</CODE>.<P>
<LI><CODE>input</CODE> gives a string; <CODE>Number(text)</CODE> turns it into a number, or
<CODE>NaN</CODE> if it can't.<P>
<LI><CODE>toFixed(2)</CODE> rounds a number to two decimal places, for money.<P>
<LI><CODE>Math</CODE> has <CODE>PI</CODE>, <CODE>sqrt</CODE>, <CODE>round</CODE> and other useful
functions.<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S9></A>
<H2>Exercises</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Without running them, work out what these print. Then check with Try.
<PRE>
print(10 - 4 - 3);
print(10 - (4 - 3));
print(17 % 5);
print(2 ** 3 ** 2);
print('3' + 4 + 5);
print(3 + 4 + '5');
</PRE>
<DT><DD>2  Write a program that asks for a temperature in Celsius and prints it in Fahrenheit. The formula is
Fahrenheit = Celsius x 9 / 5 + 32.<P>
<DT><DD>3  Change Bill so that it also prints how much the VAT would have been at today's rate of 20%.<P>
<DT><DD>4  Write a program that asks for a number of seconds and prints it in minutes and seconds, so that
200 gives <CODE>3 minutes and 20 seconds</CODE>. (Hint: <CODE>Math.floor</CODE> and <CODE>%</CODE>.)<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S10></A>
<H2>Answers</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  <CODE>3</CODE> (subtraction goes from left to right), <CODE>9</CODE>, <CODE>2</CODE>,
<CODE>512</CODE> (<CODE>**</CODE> is the exception: it goes from right to left, so this is 2 to the power
9), <CODE>345</CODE> (once there is a string, everything after it is joined), and <CODE>75</CODE> (3 + 4 is
done first, giving 7, and then the <CODE>'5'</CODE> is joined on).<P>
<DT><DD>2
<PRE>
const celsius = Number(await input('Temperature in Celsius: '));
const fahrenheit = celsius * 9 / 5 + 32;
print(`That is ${fahrenheit} degrees Fahrenheit.`);
</PRE>
<DT><DD>3  Add these lines at the end:
<PRE>
const vatNow = subtotal * 20 / 100;
print(`VAT at today's 20%: £${vatNow.toFixed(2)}`);
</PRE>
<DT><DD>4
<PRE>
const total = Number(await input('Seconds: '));
const minutes = Math.floor(total / 60);
const seconds = total % 60;
print(`${minutes} minutes and ${seconds} seconds`);
</PRE>
</DL>

